# SO-101 run sheet. E156: The $1 allowance.
# Run from the root of this checkout, against the arm registered as arm-01:
#   quackd run docs/examples/lerobot/e156/one-dollar.duck --robot arm-01 --camera-url "opencv://1?name=front" --llm openai:gpt-6-sol --max-steps 95 --no-memory
#
# max_llm_calls is one dollar at GPT-6 Sol's list price ($2 / $10 a million tokens) and the
# 15 September bench run's size of call (4,910 input and 49 output tokens), about $0.0103 a call.
# Correct it from today's first summary: 1 divided by (cost_usd / llm_calls).
---
duck: 1
name: e156-one-dollar
description: "A one-dollar allowance of LLM calls."
author: rok
requires: [move_joints]
verbs:
  allow: [report_state, move_joints, gripper, place, stop]
  confirm: []
budgets:
  max_steps: 95
  max_minutes: 30
  max_llm_calls: 95
success:
  - "You have done what the task asks, as best you can tell from your joints and any camera pictures, or said plainly why this body cannot."
abort_when:
  - Same verb fails 3 times in a row
persona: "Practical and honest: say so when you cannot do something."
---

# Task

You have an allowance of one US dollar of LLM calls, and the run stops when it is spent. Chores, in order, starting again from the top whenever you finish the list: wave, nod, point left, point right, return to neutral. Do as many chores as you can before the budget stops you, and say which chore you are on each time.

## Strategy

1. Look before you move: `report_state` gives every joint in degrees, and any camera pictures arrive with each observation.
2. Move with `move_joints` in small, deliberate steps, and use `gripper` only where the task needs the jaws.
3. Declare success with a reason that says what you did, or failure with a reason that says why you could not.
